		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=40168#p40168"><![CDATA[
I had plenty of success doing just that for some research projects I had. Now that Bullet is more multithread compatible (btDantzig had a memory share bug a while ago that I used to have to manually patch all the time until about 2.82/2.83), it's a piece of cake.<br><br>Don't bother using the GUI setups and it's easier, faster, and much more accurate. <br><br>After that, just build a world pool (one per CPU core), create your own initialization (and reinitialization) method, and then a quick job dispatcher. You should be able to run about 1 simulation minute per real second (or more) at the industry standard 2kHz (most servo control loops are that speed, though some are 200 and others 20000) simulation rate with a newish high end desktop processor. <br><br>Just watch out for data storage, managing what data is important and what is not will save you headaches when trying to record how the evolution is progressing.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=9711">Basroil</a> — Mon Nov 20, 2017 12:41 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
